WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:07.905
&gt;&gt; I am very happy to
introduce Fredrik Johansson,

00:00:07.906 --> 00:00:13.110
who is coming to us from MIT
working with [inaudible].

00:00:13.111 --> 00:00:17.250
He has been doing a lot of
variance works at forefront of

00:00:17.251 --> 00:00:20.160
the machine learning and
causal inference and

00:00:20.161 --> 00:00:23.535
I'm very much looking
forward to hearing the talk.

00:00:23.536 --> 00:00:26.745
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much.
Great to be here.

00:00:26.746 --> 00:00:28.590
Yeah, so for the last couple of

00:00:28.591 --> 00:00:30.030
years I've been thinking a lot about

00:00:30.031 --> 00:00:33.450
causal effects and
the biggest reason for that is

00:00:33.451 --> 00:00:36.180
that I'm working in something
called a clinical machine learning

00:00:36.181 --> 00:00:41.125
group at MIT and almost all of
the problems that we work with,

00:00:41.126 --> 00:00:44.495
with some exceptions, are inspired
in some way by health care.

00:00:44.496 --> 00:00:50.150
So, I'll go through a few examples
of what that might mean.

00:00:50.151 --> 00:00:53.360
One of the very first problems
that David worked on in

00:00:53.361 --> 00:00:56.750
his lab was predicting
diabetes onset and as

00:00:56.751 --> 00:00:58.910
you know that diabetes
is a very large problem

00:00:58.911 --> 00:01:01.220
here in the United States
in particular but others as

00:01:01.221 --> 00:01:06.320
well and this is your standard
prediction problem.

00:01:06.321 --> 00:01:07.850
You want to figure out
what is the risk of

00:01:07.851 --> 00:01:10.225
a person developing
type two diabetes.

00:01:10.226 --> 00:01:12.510
So, what they did in this work,

00:01:12.511 --> 00:01:14.770
I wasn't part of this but

00:01:14.771 --> 00:01:18.140
essentially look at
the personal health record

00:01:18.141 --> 00:01:20.555
or insurance record and

00:01:20.556 --> 00:01:28.220
find features that are predictive

00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:30.845
of the risk of developing
type two diabetes.

00:01:30.846 --> 00:01:33.680
So, the question here is
essentially, who is at risk?

00:01:33.681 --> 00:01:36.320
Who should we think about perhaps

00:01:36.321 --> 00:01:40.285
intervening on or
treating differently?

00:01:40.286 --> 00:01:43.640
A more recent problem that
I'm thinking about is

00:01:43.641 --> 00:01:46.800
opioid addiction which is very
relevant on the East Coast.

00:01:46.801 --> 00:01:50.100
I don't know anything about opioid
addiction on the West Coast,

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:54.590
but it's a very big problem
in the States as well

00:01:54.591 --> 00:01:58.415
and essentially what we
want to figure out here is,

00:01:58.416 --> 00:02:01.970
what about the health care system
or what about the way that we're

00:02:01.971 --> 00:02:07.080
prescribing opioids now is
driving addiction essentially.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:10.580
Over 10,000 people every year die

00:02:10.581 --> 00:02:12.830
from overdoses related
to prescription opioids.

00:02:12.831 --> 00:02:15.215
So, these are not
drugs from the street,

00:02:15.216 --> 00:02:19.500
they are potentially overdoses
that we could prevent

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:23.930
and people need pain medication

00:02:23.931 --> 00:02:26.720
but in a lot of cases we
could prescribe less.

00:02:26.721 --> 00:02:28.235
So, we know simple things like

00:02:28.236 --> 00:02:31.730
larger prescriptions mean
high risk of developing addiction

00:02:31.731 --> 00:02:34.115
that's very reasonable but
perhaps we can figure out

00:02:34.116 --> 00:02:39.085
other reasons for or
other drivers of this.

00:02:39.086 --> 00:02:41.705
Lastly, I'll mention
sepsis management

00:02:41.706 --> 00:02:43.100
which is something that a lot of

00:02:43.101 --> 00:02:44.810
people in machine learning
community and I've looked at when

00:02:44.811 --> 00:02:47.610
it comes to health care tasks.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:52.070
Sepsis is one of the leading
causes of death in

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:55.520
the intensive care unit and it's
a complication of an infection.

00:02:55.521 --> 00:03:03.095
So, essentially you can have really
horrible complications such as

00:03:03.096 --> 00:03:06.035
organ failure et cetera

00:03:06.036 --> 00:03:08.990
and one of the first things

00:03:08.991 --> 00:03:10.130
you should do obviously
when it comes to

00:03:10.131 --> 00:03:11.765
infection is to treat the infection.

00:03:11.766 --> 00:03:14.660
But a lot of the things that we
do when we treat patients with

00:03:14.661 --> 00:03:17.865
sepsis is also just management.

00:03:17.866 --> 00:03:20.740
Things like the
blood pressure, the artery,

00:03:20.741 --> 00:03:23.690
the oxygen intake can all be
affected by this and this

00:03:23.691 --> 00:03:26.830
is the more continuous task
of managing patients.

00:03:26.831 --> 00:03:30.220
We have to make sure that all these
things are in the right range.

00:03:30.221 --> 00:03:34.150
So, for example, if
your oxygen drops too low you,

00:03:34.151 --> 00:03:36.505
might want to put the patient
on a mechanical ventilator

00:03:36.506 --> 00:03:39.110
and if you do that you might want
to sedate the patient et cetera.

00:03:39.111 --> 00:03:42.580
So, this is more of a sequential
process and we want

00:03:42.581 --> 00:03:43.720
to manage these patients so that

00:03:43.721 --> 00:03:46.090
their long-term quality of life is

00:03:46.091 --> 00:03:47.980
good as opposed to just
making them survive

00:03:47.981 --> 00:03:50.320
the first 24 or 48 hours or whatever

00:03:50.321 --> 00:03:52.570
they spend in the ICU because

00:03:52.571 --> 00:03:55.880
a lot of the interventions that
we do have long-term effects.

00:03:55.881 --> 00:03:58.660
So, these three problems illustrate

00:03:58.661 --> 00:04:00.940
essentially three different modes
of applying machine

00:04:00.941 --> 00:04:03.940
learning in health care
and the first one being

00:04:03.941 --> 00:04:05.490
a very standard
prediction problem that

00:04:05.491 --> 00:04:07.775
I'm sure most of you have worked with

00:04:07.776 --> 00:04:12.560
a lot and essentially
figuring out who's at risk of

00:04:12.561 --> 00:04:15.230
diabetes onset is something
that we can do using

00:04:15.231 --> 00:04:18.770
just supervised learning
or prediction methods.

00:04:18.771 --> 00:04:21.560
But the second one,
figuring out what are

00:04:21.561 --> 00:04:23.870
the drivers of opioid
medication and how can

00:04:23.871 --> 00:04:26.300
we prescribe opioids differently

00:04:26.301 --> 00:04:29.000
so that we reduce the risk
of developing addiction,

00:04:29.001 --> 00:04:30.890
that's more of the causal problems

00:04:30.891 --> 00:04:32.645
that I will be focusing on today.

00:04:32.646 --> 00:04:35.300
Essentially, we might be
able to or we might consider

00:04:35.301 --> 00:04:38.060
choosing between a synthetic
and unnatural opioid,

00:04:38.061 --> 00:04:40.619
I should say medication,

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:44.240
and we're interested in
what is the contrast

00:04:44.241 --> 00:04:47.915
between the responses under
these two choices of medication.

00:04:47.916 --> 00:04:49.850
The last one is more of

00:04:49.851 --> 00:04:52.670
a sequential decision-making problem

00:04:52.671 --> 00:04:55.070
or reinforcement learning problem
if we want to treat it like

00:04:55.071 --> 00:04:58.460
that but we make a series
of choices where

00:04:58.461 --> 00:04:59.600
each choice depends on

00:04:59.601 --> 00:05:03.180
the previous choices but also what
happened as a result of them.

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:10.790
We can think of this as a slightly
more complicated version of

00:05:10.791 --> 00:05:12.440
this in other cases but

00:05:12.441 --> 00:05:15.410
we're also interested in
slightly different quantity.

00:05:15.411 --> 00:05:18.020
We want to figure out
what's a good policy,

00:05:18.021 --> 00:05:19.370
a good reactive policy for it.

00:05:19.371 --> 00:05:21.155
If we see a patient
in a certain state,

00:05:21.156 --> 00:05:22.800
what should we do which is slightly

00:05:22.801 --> 00:05:25.185
different from this where
in which we might consider.

00:05:25.186 --> 00:05:27.200
If I choose between these two things

00:05:27.201 --> 00:05:29.375
for everyone in the population,
what would happen.

00:05:29.376 --> 00:05:31.460
Whereas in the policy
setting we might not

00:05:31.461 --> 00:05:35.335
consider the whole range
of actions for everyone.

00:05:35.336 --> 00:05:40.550
So, the broad theme of
this talk will be to predict

00:05:40.551 --> 00:05:42.830
effects of decisions and

00:05:42.831 --> 00:05:45.260
we need somehow
causal reasoning to do this.

00:05:45.261 --> 00:05:46.490
This is what I argue on,

00:05:46.491 --> 00:05:51.780
I'm sure you've heard this argument
before from others here and

00:05:51.781 --> 00:05:56.780
the main thing that I've
been thinking a lot about

00:05:56.781 --> 00:05:59.030
recently is that we have a lot
of high dimensional data in

00:05:59.031 --> 00:06:01.490
health care and it's not only
because of the sequential problem,

00:06:01.491 --> 00:06:05.450
but also the fact that we
have diagnosis information,

00:06:05.451 --> 00:06:06.830
we have other kinds of

00:06:06.831 --> 00:06:11.660
medical history and we
have a bunch of symptoms,

00:06:11.661 --> 00:06:14.150
we have genetic information,
in some cases we have X-rays,

00:06:14.151 --> 00:06:15.530
a lot of the things
that contribute to

00:06:15.531 --> 00:06:18.570
a high-dimensional data regime.

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:23.240
Then the second part of this talk
will be essentially linking this

00:06:23.241 --> 00:06:24.950
to domain adaptation which
is something that I've

00:06:24.951 --> 00:06:27.095
done successfully in the past.

00:06:27.096 --> 00:06:28.640
Domain adaptation,
if you know, is part

00:06:28.641 --> 00:06:30.560
of transfer learning
or an instance of

00:06:30.561 --> 00:06:33.770
transfer learning and it

00:06:33.771 --> 00:06:36.950
has a lot of similarities with
causal effect estimation.

00:06:36.951 --> 00:06:40.185
So, the last part is
an open question but

00:06:40.186 --> 00:06:44.645
recently I was working on
a product of essentially

00:06:44.646 --> 00:06:46.160
trying to figure out what would go

00:06:46.161 --> 00:06:47.990
wrong if we apply something like

00:06:47.991 --> 00:06:49.955
reinforcement learning
without thinking too much

00:06:49.956 --> 00:06:52.470
in healthcare problem
and specifically,

00:06:52.471 --> 00:06:54.980
I will be talking causally
about what's called off policy

00:06:54.981 --> 00:06:57.275
or observational settings where

00:06:57.276 --> 00:06:58.850
we can't run an experiment
is essentially.

00:06:58.851 --> 00:07:01.490
So, in those settings what should

00:07:01.491 --> 00:07:02.810
we try to figure out about

00:07:02.811 --> 00:07:06.030
our policies before we
apply them in practice.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:12.080
So, the types of problems
that we think most about in

00:07:12.081 --> 00:07:16.610
our group is something like
this or at least I do.

00:07:16.611 --> 00:07:18.620
There's a lot of work on
sequential things as well.

00:07:18.621 --> 00:07:20.470
We'll get there a bit later.

00:07:20.471 --> 00:07:23.420
So, we can imagine a patient
coming into the hospital,

00:07:23.421 --> 00:07:28.460
she's 54 years old and female
and she has high blood sugar.

00:07:28.461 --> 00:07:30.850
So, this could be
a pre-diabetic patient.

00:07:30.851 --> 00:07:32.540
We have to figure out
how can we manage

00:07:32.541 --> 00:07:34.925
her blood sugars so that it's lower

00:07:34.926 --> 00:07:37.400
a few months down the line and we can

00:07:37.401 --> 00:07:40.940
consider choosing between
two different medications to do so.

00:07:40.941 --> 00:07:44.795
The question then is
a few months down the line,

00:07:44.796 --> 00:07:46.475
which of these medications will have

00:07:46.476 --> 00:07:49.055
a better effect on her blood sugar

00:07:49.056 --> 00:07:51.590
and these will be what
we call the potential

00:07:51.591 --> 00:07:54.340
outcomes of this choice.

00:07:54.341 --> 00:07:56.300
So, I will denote here by

00:07:56.301 --> 00:07:59.465
Y0 the outcome that we
observe if we were to give

00:07:59.466 --> 00:08:02.990
medication A and Y1 the opposite

00:08:02.991 --> 00:08:05.510
of the outcome under medication B.

00:08:05.511 --> 00:08:07.880
So, in a lot of cases we

00:08:07.881 --> 00:08:10.775
could perform an experiment
to try and figure this out.

00:08:10.776 --> 00:08:12.860
We can gather a bunch of

00:08:12.861 --> 00:08:14.610
pre-diabetic patients and then test

00:08:14.611 --> 00:08:16.520
these two medications
separately and figure

00:08:16.521 --> 00:08:19.670
out what was the average response

00:08:19.671 --> 00:08:22.435
or even conditional response
in these groups.

00:08:22.436 --> 00:08:27.410
But for a lot of cases we can't
do that and it could be because

00:08:27.411 --> 00:08:32.870
the patients are hard to
recruit or might be expensive,

00:08:32.871 --> 00:08:37.040
but there are also some types
of interventions that we just

00:08:37.041 --> 00:08:38.690
can't randomize like forcing

00:08:38.691 --> 00:08:41.820
patients to smoke to see the
effect of that et cetera.

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:45.350
So, the typical dataset we

00:08:45.351 --> 00:08:47.420
will be thinking about
is something like this.

00:08:47.421 --> 00:08:49.160
We have some descriptors about

00:08:49.161 --> 00:08:51.995
the patients like their age
and blood pressure et cetera,

00:08:51.996 --> 00:08:54.290
and we have essentially
a column recording

00:08:54.291 --> 00:08:57.835
which treatment they
received previously.

00:08:57.836 --> 00:09:01.010
We know what their blood sugar
was a few months down the line.

00:09:01.011 --> 00:09:02.960
We want to figure out what

00:09:02.961 --> 00:09:06.060
among these choices of
treatment was the better one.

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:08.810
The problem of course
is that we can only

00:09:08.811 --> 00:09:11.175
observe the outcome under
one of these treatments.

00:09:11.176 --> 00:09:13.010
So, what we would like
is essentially to fill

00:09:13.011 --> 00:09:15.950
in these missing cells in

00:09:15.951 --> 00:09:20.460
this table and crucially

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:24.870
these cells are not
missing at random. Yeah.

00:09:24.871 --> 00:09:28.625
&gt;&gt; Do we also the
pre-treatment of blood sugar?

00:09:28.626 --> 00:09:30.960
&gt;&gt; Yeah. That can be. Exactly.

00:09:30.961 --> 00:09:33.120
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]

00:09:33.121 --> 00:09:35.190
&gt;&gt; Oh, sorry. The question was,

00:09:35.191 --> 00:09:39.285
do we also know the blood sugar

00:09:39.286 --> 00:09:41.550
prior to treatment and yeah

00:09:41.551 --> 00:09:44.640
that would be a good feature
to collect for sure.

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:51.265
Yeah, so essentially this slide
is meant to illustrate that

00:09:51.266 --> 00:09:55.610
these cells that we want
to fill in we can't treat

00:09:55.611 --> 00:09:57.620
them as the standard
prediction problem

00:09:57.621 --> 00:10:00.240
in the sense that they're
not missing at random.

00:10:00.241 --> 00:10:04.550
The observations that we have

00:10:04.551 --> 00:10:06.500
are not representative
of the observations

00:10:06.501 --> 00:10:09.405
that we want necessarily.

00:10:09.406 --> 00:10:13.840
So that will be the goal of
this talk to figure them out.

00:10:13.841 --> 00:10:17.620
So, I will introduce some notation
to talk about these things

00:10:17.621 --> 00:10:21.100
and the first being X big feature.

00:10:21.101 --> 00:10:22.810
Feature vector essentially or

00:10:22.811 --> 00:10:24.700
covariate representation,
this could be an image,

00:10:24.701 --> 00:10:28.570
this could be some insurance
claim information.

00:10:28.571 --> 00:10:30.100
We have treatment assignments T,

00:10:30.101 --> 00:10:32.350
which for the majority of

00:10:32.351 --> 00:10:35.320
the talk I will assume is
binary and choice of A or B.

00:10:35.321 --> 00:10:38.770
We have potential outcomes under
these two different treatments.

00:10:38.771 --> 00:10:42.100
This will be Y(0) and Y(1)
and our goal is to figure out

00:10:42.101 --> 00:10:48.835
the outcome under an intervention
for specific patient X,

00:10:48.836 --> 00:10:51.880
where a patient represented
by feature X or

00:10:51.881 --> 00:10:54.100
the difference between
two interventions

00:10:54.101 --> 00:10:55.885
or the outcomes under
each two interventions.

00:10:55.886 --> 00:10:58.180
I think that's easier
if I just show what I

00:10:58.181 --> 00:11:00.955
mean in terms or using a figure.

00:11:00.956 --> 00:11:06.235
This here could be the outcome
under the first intervention,

00:11:06.236 --> 00:11:07.915
the control outcome
if you would like.

00:11:07.916 --> 00:11:09.655
A function of X, for example,

00:11:09.656 --> 00:11:12.160
age and I've put mortality
on the x-axis here.

00:11:12.161 --> 00:11:16.310
It's likely that you are more at
risk of death when you're older.

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:19.330
The question is can we use

00:11:19.331 --> 00:11:22.570
a treatment to sort of reduce
this risk of mortality?

00:11:22.571 --> 00:11:24.880
That might be the case, we can have

00:11:24.881 --> 00:11:27.370
a function that represents

00:11:27.371 --> 00:11:29.305
the outcome under treatment
that looks something like this.

00:11:29.306 --> 00:11:35.545
These are just invented, but this
is not unreasonable to assume.

00:11:35.546 --> 00:11:38.860
If you're at high enough edge,
there's nothing we can do,

00:11:38.861 --> 00:11:41.140
but perhaps in the sort

00:11:41.141 --> 00:11:43.870
of middle region here
the treatment has an effect.

00:11:43.871 --> 00:11:46.960
What you can see is essentially
a couple of things,

00:11:46.961 --> 00:11:51.325
the effect of choosing the treatment.

00:11:51.326 --> 00:11:53.320
Treatment one over
treatment zero seems to

00:11:53.321 --> 00:11:55.225
have an effect in some
regions but not in all.

00:11:55.226 --> 00:11:58.645
So we have what's called a
heterogeneous treatment effect.

00:11:58.646 --> 00:12:02.320
But also it seems to be mostly
sort of beneficial, the treatment,

00:12:02.321 --> 00:12:04.930
like this difference between
these two lines which

00:12:04.931 --> 00:12:07.540
is what we're interested
in is mostly negative.

00:12:07.541 --> 00:12:11.690
So you reduce the mortality
if you choose treatment one.

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:15.970
We don't observe these lines.

00:12:15.971 --> 00:12:19.240
These curves are the goal

00:12:19.241 --> 00:12:22.030
with this study or those are
where we want to find out,

00:12:22.031 --> 00:12:26.260
but we observe only a skewed
sample of these curves.

00:12:26.261 --> 00:12:28.570
It's illustrated with a table before.

00:12:28.571 --> 00:12:31.210
We might have a situation
something like this,

00:12:31.211 --> 00:12:35.350
where we have a treated group.

00:12:35.351 --> 00:12:37.840
This should be zero and one,

00:12:37.841 --> 00:12:39.715
and that, one and zero by the way.

00:12:39.716 --> 00:12:42.700
A treated group that's
a little skewed to

00:12:42.701 --> 00:12:44.590
the older population
here that you're more

00:12:44.591 --> 00:12:46.720
likely to receive
treatment if you're older,

00:12:46.721 --> 00:12:50.890
and a control group which is
younger, and even worse than this,

00:12:50.891 --> 00:12:54.160
we don't observe the curves in

00:12:54.161 --> 00:12:55.900
the support of these
densities either we observe

00:12:55.901 --> 00:12:58.720
samples from these densities.

00:12:58.721 --> 00:13:01.570
Essentially, we might
see something like this,

00:13:01.571 --> 00:13:03.310
where we have a bunch of

00:13:03.311 --> 00:13:05.740
younger patients that
didn't receive treatment.

00:13:05.741 --> 00:13:08.635
These are the blue dots here
following the solid curve,

00:13:08.636 --> 00:13:10.540
and we have a bunch

00:13:10.541 --> 00:13:13.615
of treated patients that
are a little bit older.

00:13:13.616 --> 00:13:17.259
If you look now at
the average response

00:13:17.260 --> 00:13:20.215
to treatment or the average outcome
in these two different groups,

00:13:20.216 --> 00:13:23.020
we see essentially that the average
for the treated population is

00:13:23.021 --> 00:13:26.660
higher and the average for
the control population slower.

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:28.960
What we concluded before was that

00:13:28.961 --> 00:13:31.075
the treatment was always beneficial.

00:13:31.076 --> 00:13:33.039
So it's kind of surprising

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:35.500
or not if you've seen
an example like this before,

00:13:35.501 --> 00:13:37.660
but it's where you can

00:13:37.661 --> 00:13:39.970
see is essentially that these
average do not tell the story that

00:13:39.971 --> 00:13:45.430
we're interested in because here
it appears that the effect Tau or

00:13:45.431 --> 00:13:47.380
the difference between
these two outcomes

00:13:47.381 --> 00:13:51.880
is adverse on average as
opposed to beneficial.

00:13:51.881 --> 00:13:54.955
This is an instance of compounding.

00:13:54.956 --> 00:13:57.550
This age here influences not

00:13:57.551 --> 00:14:01.190
only the response to treatment
but also the treatment itself.

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:05.740
We have a few problems
essentially as far as I'm

00:14:05.741 --> 00:14:08.110
concerned, we have compounding,

00:14:08.111 --> 00:14:10.195
which is what I've just
illustrated that if we

00:14:10.196 --> 00:14:12.940
look at the just the sample
averages that we receive,

00:14:12.941 --> 00:14:15.190
we get the wrong idea of the effect.

00:14:15.191 --> 00:14:17.320
We also have an overlap problem which

00:14:17.321 --> 00:14:19.570
I didn't talk too
much about it before,

00:14:19.571 --> 00:14:23.245
but you can see that these two
groups are essentially disjoint,

00:14:23.246 --> 00:14:26.000
and we have to work
with that somehow.

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:30.460
The typical way to
solve these problems

00:14:30.461 --> 00:14:32.650
in causal inference at least papers,

00:14:32.651 --> 00:14:33.970
I hope not in practice,

00:14:33.971 --> 00:14:37.610
is to assume that these do not
exist, these two problems.

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:44.550
We will talk a lot about this
going forward, but essentially,

00:14:44.551 --> 00:14:47.340
that the first assumption that people

00:14:47.341 --> 00:14:48.840
tend to make in
these kind of situations

00:14:48.841 --> 00:14:50.690
is something called ignorability,

00:14:50.691 --> 00:14:54.190
in which you assume
that if you know X,

00:14:54.191 --> 00:14:59.020
if have enough variables

00:14:59.021 --> 00:15:01.960
to predict essentially the
treatment assignment well,

00:15:01.961 --> 00:15:04.885
the two potential outcomes that you

00:15:04.886 --> 00:15:07.880
are interested in should be
independent of that treatment.

00:15:07.950 --> 00:15:11.710
So if you control for X, we
can estimate the effect Tau.

00:15:11.711 --> 00:15:14.680
The other one is common support,
saying essentially that,

00:15:14.681 --> 00:15:17.980
at any point along the
axis that I showed before,

00:15:17.981 --> 00:15:20.125
at any point in the covariate space,

00:15:20.126 --> 00:15:23.170
we have some probability of
receiving any of the treatments.

00:15:23.171 --> 00:15:25.060
So if we want to compare
the difference between

00:15:25.061 --> 00:15:27.130
two treatments or the outcomes
on the two treatments,

00:15:27.131 --> 00:15:29.845
we would need of course to
have samples from each,

00:15:29.846 --> 00:15:34.040
and this is what I'll talk
most about I think today.

00:15:35.730 --> 00:15:38.410
There are more assumptions
that you need,

00:15:38.411 --> 00:15:41.305
like IID assumptions is one of them.

00:15:41.306 --> 00:15:43.180
This consistency is another,

00:15:43.181 --> 00:15:49.270
it is saying essentially that
if you prescribe treatment T,

00:15:49.271 --> 00:15:51.760
you will observe the outcome
under that treatment,

00:15:51.761 --> 00:15:53.350
and this is not always true.

00:15:53.351 --> 00:15:56.260
If a patient elects not to take
the treatment prescribed then

00:15:56.261 --> 00:15:59.560
you're not going to see
that outcome clearly.

00:15:59.561 --> 00:16:01.510
I will take this for granted

00:16:01.511 --> 00:16:03.890
for now and I will talk
about the other ones.

00:16:07.650 --> 00:16:09.850
We still have observed compounding,

00:16:09.851 --> 00:16:14.950
we still have our variables X
that we need to control for.

00:16:14.951 --> 00:16:19.300
Roughly speaking, what we would
like is to figure out what

00:16:19.301 --> 00:16:23.590
is the observed outcome under
treatment for patient X,

00:16:23.591 --> 00:16:24.955
but we only have

00:16:24.956 --> 00:16:28.285
this function for the people who
over this receive that treatment.

00:16:28.286 --> 00:16:31.465
We only see the outcome under
treatment for the treated.

00:16:31.466 --> 00:16:33.400
So how do we connect these two?

00:16:33.401 --> 00:16:35.935
Well, there are a couple of
different solutions to that.

00:16:35.936 --> 00:16:38.260
Essentially, if you have the two
conditions that I showed before,

00:16:38.261 --> 00:16:40.090
there are suite of methods
that you can apply

00:16:40.091 --> 00:16:46.550
to cross this, sort of divide.

00:16:47.190 --> 00:16:50.965
Three of the most
classical ones are these.

00:16:50.966 --> 00:16:53.470
Regression, essentially
fits a function to

00:16:53.471 --> 00:16:55.360
each potential outcome or

00:16:55.361 --> 00:16:59.755
each observed outcome under
each treatment, and re-weighting.

00:16:59.756 --> 00:17:01.060
I'm sure you've seen before in

00:17:01.061 --> 00:17:02.740
other contexts if you
haven't done this,

00:17:02.741 --> 00:17:06.200
essentially doing
important sampling to figure out

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:09.700
how do we find a
representative sample

00:17:09.701 --> 00:17:12.295
of the whole population from
the biased sample that we have,

00:17:12.296 --> 00:17:14.935
matching just pairing up
essentially people with

00:17:14.936 --> 00:17:17.320
similar covariates to
figure out what would be

00:17:17.321 --> 00:17:21.025
their counterfactual outcome if
they had the opposite treatment.

00:17:21.026 --> 00:17:25.150
I will talk mostly about
regression estimators today,

00:17:25.151 --> 00:17:28.750
and under the two assumptions
that we had before,

00:17:28.751 --> 00:17:33.910
we only have to do regression
to solve our problem with

00:17:33.911 --> 00:17:35.530
some caveats in the sense
that we might not

00:17:35.531 --> 00:17:39.100
have the amount of
samples that we need,

00:17:39.101 --> 00:17:42.730
but essentially, under
those conditions you get that

00:17:42.731 --> 00:17:44.830
the potential outcome
under a certain treatment

00:17:44.831 --> 00:17:47.990
is equal to the conditional outcome.

00:17:51.270 --> 00:17:53.650
You can do this in a lot
of different ways,

00:17:53.651 --> 00:17:55.150
you can solve these kinds of

00:17:55.151 --> 00:17:56.800
regression problems in
a lot of different ways.

00:17:56.801 --> 00:17:58.480
The most simple one or the most

00:17:58.481 --> 00:18:00.310
perhaps intuitive for
someone who comes from

00:18:00.311 --> 00:18:03.310
a machine learning background
is to treat these two curves as

00:18:03.311 --> 00:18:04.855
two different prediction problems

00:18:04.856 --> 00:18:07.270
to solve or to two different
estimation problems.

00:18:07.271 --> 00:18:10.390
You can try and fit the blue curve
separately and the orange curve

00:18:10.391 --> 00:18:13.180
separately and take
their or construct

00:18:13.181 --> 00:18:16.060
their difference to
get your prediction,

00:18:16.061 --> 00:18:18.880
but there are also methods
specifically designed to estimate

00:18:18.881 --> 00:18:24.080
the difference at once and not
solve the two prediction problems.

00:18:24.330 --> 00:18:26.530
Our problem remains that

00:18:26.531 --> 00:18:28.855
if our two treatment groups
are very different,

00:18:28.856 --> 00:18:30.685
for example, if we
didn't have overlap,

00:18:30.686 --> 00:18:34.840
solving these estimation
problems is not going be

00:18:34.841 --> 00:18:39.310
easy at least in
the sense that the error

00:18:39.311 --> 00:18:41.230
that we have on the data
that we observed is not

00:18:41.231 --> 00:18:43.180
going to be representative
of the error

00:18:43.181 --> 00:18:45.850
that we expect on a

00:18:45.851 --> 00:18:47.890
different or counterfactual
intervention if you like,

00:18:47.891 --> 00:18:50.000
where we assign
the opposite treatment.

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:55.630
There are a few different problems
that make this hard,

00:18:55.631 --> 00:18:58.660
and the most relevant to this talk

00:18:58.661 --> 00:19:02.335
is that we have
often high-dimensional data.

00:19:02.336 --> 00:19:05.920
There are essentially
two aspects of this

00:19:05.921 --> 00:19:10.870
that have basically
the opposite calculations.

00:19:10.871 --> 00:19:15.130
One is this idea that if
we measure more variables,

00:19:15.131 --> 00:19:17.380
we're less likely to have
left out something that we

00:19:17.381 --> 00:19:20.770
need to adjust for compounding.

00:19:20.771 --> 00:19:24.520
This is true, but I put an asterisk
here in the sense that we're

00:19:24.521 --> 00:19:25.900
not guaranteed to have less bias

00:19:25.901 --> 00:19:27.730
necessarily if we
include more variables.

00:19:27.731 --> 00:19:29.695
This is a little technical,

00:19:29.696 --> 00:19:31.750
but you can show essentially that If

00:19:31.751 --> 00:19:33.880
you include variables
that you shouldn't have,

00:19:33.881 --> 00:19:37.960
you will introduce more bias
than if you left them out.

00:19:37.961 --> 00:19:41.050
The most famous example of
that I think is M-bias,

00:19:41.051 --> 00:19:45.445
which is I'm not going to get
into what that is exactly,

00:19:45.446 --> 00:19:49.570
but another one would be where
you condition on a variable that

00:19:49.571 --> 00:19:51.490
happened as a result of treatment or

00:19:51.491 --> 00:19:54.190
after treatment as opposed before,

00:19:54.191 --> 00:19:57.550
so you can introduce selection
bias and that kind of thing.

00:19:57.551 --> 00:20:00.550
But this I am sure you're much more

00:20:00.551 --> 00:20:02.080
aware of all the time

00:20:02.081 --> 00:20:03.760
because whenever we have
high dimensionality,

00:20:03.761 --> 00:20:05.995
we have to struggle
with sample sizes.

00:20:05.996 --> 00:20:08.200
In this case, we also
have a different kind

00:20:08.201 --> 00:20:10.585
of curse of high dimensionality
in the sense that,

00:20:10.586 --> 00:20:12.805
it's less likely there
are two treatment groups

00:20:12.806 --> 00:20:16.210
overlap as we call it when
we have more variables.

00:20:16.211 --> 00:20:20.430
So if you think about the matching
problem that I flashed before,

00:20:20.431 --> 00:20:22.160
if you want to find someone who's

00:20:22.161 --> 00:20:24.160
very similar to you with
had the opposite treatment,

00:20:24.161 --> 00:20:25.880
that gets less and less

00:20:25.881 --> 00:20:28.550
likely the more variables you
have, or said differently,

00:20:28.551 --> 00:20:30.170
you could say that everyone

00:20:30.171 --> 00:20:32.600
becomes roughly equally
comparable when you

00:20:32.601 --> 00:20:34.520
have a lot of data or a lot
of dimensions I should

00:20:34.521 --> 00:20:37.920
say, which is not what we want.

00:20:38.340 --> 00:20:40.360
So, the question then is,

00:20:40.361 --> 00:20:44.020
how do we mitigate this curse
of dimensionality?

00:20:44.021 --> 00:20:47.500
Specifically, what I've
been thinking about is,

00:20:47.501 --> 00:20:49.930
can we learn a
representation or can we

00:20:49.931 --> 00:20:52.870
find a representation
of our data fee?

00:20:52.871 --> 00:20:55.540
Such that we still have
the two assumptions that

00:20:55.541 --> 00:20:58.420
we needed for estimating
causal effects,

00:20:58.421 --> 00:21:00.010
we still need ignorability,

00:21:00.011 --> 00:21:02.860
we needed to address for

00:21:02.861 --> 00:21:06.550
compounding and we also need
a common support of course,

00:21:06.551 --> 00:21:08.620
but if we find a
representation that's

00:21:08.621 --> 00:21:10.615
smaller than the original space,

00:21:10.616 --> 00:21:11.815
there's a chance that we can sort of

00:21:11.816 --> 00:21:13.480
bump up the margins
on this a little bit.

00:21:13.481 --> 00:21:15.670
We can find a better support

00:21:15.671 --> 00:21:19.430
or better overlap than
what we had before.

00:21:19.500 --> 00:21:27.190
So, if you think of this as being
a large number of covariates,

00:21:27.191 --> 00:21:28.780
for example, that we
want to adjust for,

00:21:28.781 --> 00:21:30.250
just removing some of

00:21:30.251 --> 00:21:32.440
the variables would be
the easiest way to achieve this.

00:21:32.441 --> 00:21:35.630
We have a better chance
of having overlap.

00:21:35.700 --> 00:21:41.800
So, I've thought about
this problem for a while.

00:21:41.801 --> 00:21:43.870
A few years ago,

00:21:43.871 --> 00:21:45.670
I started working on this and what

00:21:45.671 --> 00:21:50.590
we did essentially at
that point was to starting,

00:21:50.591 --> 00:21:53.170
draw comparisons between
causal effect estimation

00:21:53.171 --> 00:21:55.330
and domain adaptation. Go ahead.

00:21:55.331 --> 00:21:58.720
&gt;&gt; Is there, I

00:21:58.721 --> 00:22:00.910
don't know like then quickly
illustrate an example to

00:22:00.911 --> 00:22:03.580
see if our fee affects
might have the ignorability

00:22:03.581 --> 00:22:06.850
like in your mortality example
like we'll see that okay,

00:22:06.851 --> 00:22:09.010
maybe we'll start
discretizing it or I think

00:22:09.011 --> 00:22:12.505
then we'll obviously
get with their overlap.

00:22:12.506 --> 00:22:16.570
&gt;&gt; So the question was,
is there an example

00:22:16.571 --> 00:22:18.370
that illustrates how fee can

00:22:18.371 --> 00:22:20.650
be useful for
ignorability or helpful?

00:22:20.651 --> 00:22:22.750
It's the opposite.

00:22:22.751 --> 00:22:24.610
Whenever we remove information

00:22:24.611 --> 00:22:26.380
that it's less likely that this will

00:22:26.381 --> 00:22:30.610
hold except for the biases
that I mentioned before.

00:22:30.611 --> 00:22:34.360
But yeah, so that is
actually the problem.

00:22:34.361 --> 00:22:36.670
We want to find fee to achieve this,

00:22:36.671 --> 00:22:38.830
but this is the constraint
that we have essentially.

00:22:38.831 --> 00:22:42.970
&gt;&gt; So let me

00:22:42.971 --> 00:22:48.505
think about not having [inaudible]
things that are maybe really biased,

00:22:48.506 --> 00:22:55.615
then would I effectively be
asking what is the largest,

00:22:55.616 --> 00:22:58.765
extra smallest satisfying
notability already?

00:22:58.766 --> 00:23:01.405
I'm just asking what is
the largest fee I can work with

00:23:01.406 --> 00:23:04.665
getting [inaudible] or good enough?

00:23:04.666 --> 00:23:07.990
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So the question
again for the,

00:23:07.991 --> 00:23:10.210
sorry for the recording is,

00:23:10.211 --> 00:23:12.970
oh man. Sorry, I wish
I could [inaudible].

00:23:12.971 --> 00:23:15.760
Yes.

00:23:15.761 --> 00:23:17.200
Essentially, should I try and get

00:23:17.201 --> 00:23:20.305
the largest fees so that I
have common support or better,

00:23:20.306 --> 00:23:24.220
let's say, better chance
of getting common support?

00:23:24.221 --> 00:23:27.325
But so that I still believe
that I have ignorability.

00:23:27.326 --> 00:23:29.980
Yeah. I think that's
a reasonable way to think about it.

00:23:29.981 --> 00:23:32.005
This will be a trade off for sure.

00:23:32.006 --> 00:23:33.340
Essentially, you can
think of this as variance

00:23:33.341 --> 00:23:35.420
and this as bias, right?

00:23:37.170 --> 00:23:39.640
In some cases, you

00:23:39.641 --> 00:23:42.970
might have an easier time
achieving this than you might,

00:23:42.971 --> 00:23:44.230
that you might be more

00:23:44.231 --> 00:23:47.150
concerned about keeping
information that are moving in.

00:23:47.160 --> 00:23:50.995
I don't have a great general
answer to that question,

00:23:50.996 --> 00:23:54.730
but I think that's
a reasonable approach to take.

00:23:54.731 --> 00:23:58.150
Maybe after we see some algorithm or

00:23:58.151 --> 00:24:00.070
theory that you might have

00:24:00.071 --> 00:24:02.245
a better idea of that,
I hope that's the case.

00:24:02.246 --> 00:24:04.600
So, we started thinking
about domain adaptation

00:24:04.601 --> 00:24:06.880
because turns out is
very similar to what I'm

00:24:06.881 --> 00:24:10.375
talking about and I

00:24:10.376 --> 00:24:12.670
used to just say what I learned
from domain adaptation here,

00:24:12.671 --> 00:24:15.400
but essentially, I've
changed my mind a lot on on

00:24:15.401 --> 00:24:18.985
the topic of domain adaptation
to working with this problem.

00:24:18.986 --> 00:24:22.030
So, we will consider just one side of

00:24:22.031 --> 00:24:25.090
the causal effect
estimation problem from

00:24:25.091 --> 00:24:30.670
before and essentially we will
try and predict this blue curve,

00:24:30.671 --> 00:24:34.405
there's outcome under
control medication

00:24:34.406 --> 00:24:35.815
for the treated population.

00:24:35.816 --> 00:24:37.180
So for the red points,
we want to infer

00:24:37.181 --> 00:24:39.860
what is their sort of
location on the blue.

00:24:41.670 --> 00:24:46.480
Doing this one-sided
problem or solving

00:24:46.481 --> 00:24:48.190
that one-sided problem
turns out to be the same as

00:24:48.191 --> 00:24:51.385
doing what's called
unsupervised domain adaptation,

00:24:51.386 --> 00:24:53.620
and in that specific formulation.

00:24:53.621 --> 00:24:58.165
So domain adaptation is essentially
the idea that you want to

00:24:58.166 --> 00:25:00.580
learn a model or fit a model on

00:25:00.581 --> 00:25:03.265
one type of data and then
apply it to something else,

00:25:03.266 --> 00:25:07.040
or one distribution of data
and apply it somewhere else.

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:10.680
The data that you have essentially in

00:25:10.681 --> 00:25:13.845
the specifically unsupervised
domain adaptation formulation

00:25:13.846 --> 00:25:20.020
is labels from what's called
a source domain and features from

00:25:20.021 --> 00:25:21.640
that domain and then I have

00:25:21.641 --> 00:25:24.550
only features from another domain
called the target domain.

00:25:24.551 --> 00:25:26.800
So you have features and labels from

00:25:26.801 --> 00:25:29.530
the source and only
features from the target.

00:25:29.531 --> 00:25:31.905
So, these map do

00:25:31.906 --> 00:25:34.800
counter-factual
prediction in the sense

00:25:34.801 --> 00:25:37.350
that these are your
factual control outcomes,

00:25:37.351 --> 00:25:38.715
the outcomes that you observe,

00:25:38.716 --> 00:25:40.560
and you have your treated group,

00:25:40.561 --> 00:25:46.270
but not treated labels essentially
based on the slide that I

00:25:46.271 --> 00:25:49.180
showed a minute ago and
your goal is to figure

00:25:49.181 --> 00:25:52.690
out the labels for
the unlabeled group.

00:25:52.691 --> 00:25:55.420
The assumptions are very
similar in these two problems,

00:25:55.421 --> 00:25:56.590
the counterfactual
prediction and demand

00:25:56.591 --> 00:25:58.120
adaptation in the sense that we

00:25:58.121 --> 00:26:01.225
have this ignorability condition
still that I showed before,

00:26:01.226 --> 00:26:02.290
but it would be called something like

00:26:02.291 --> 00:26:03.685
covariate shift in domain adaptation.

00:26:03.686 --> 00:26:05.560
It's usually written
slightly differently,

00:26:05.561 --> 00:26:08.785
but the idea is essentially that
the labeling function that we

00:26:08.786 --> 00:26:10.090
want to learn and apply in

00:26:10.091 --> 00:26:12.460
a different context doesn't
change between domains.

00:26:12.461 --> 00:26:14.050
So, p of y given x.

00:26:14.051 --> 00:26:19.220
If you like, it's fixed when you
go from one domain to the next.

00:26:21.270 --> 00:26:26.515
One of the most often considered
tasks in domain adaptation,

00:26:26.516 --> 00:26:28.450
at least when it comes to
the machine learning community,

00:26:28.451 --> 00:26:31.900
is image classification essentially.

00:26:31.901 --> 00:26:35.035
This is a benchmark that people
are very comfortable with

00:26:35.036 --> 00:26:40.090
and these illustrate
two different benchmark problems

00:26:40.091 --> 00:26:42.590
that are often used.

00:26:43.350 --> 00:26:45.520
Here on the top row,

00:26:45.521 --> 00:26:47.890
we see, well, I don't
know if you can see it,

00:26:47.891 --> 00:26:51.925
but it's digits from house numbers
that have been cropped.

00:26:51.926 --> 00:26:57.610
So, essentially, the center digit
is the label of the image.

00:26:57.611 --> 00:26:59.710
So seven, three, and zero, I think,

00:26:59.711 --> 00:27:04.630
and the bottom row here is MNIST
that you've seen a million times.

00:27:04.631 --> 00:27:07.840
But what's happening here
is essentially that we use

00:27:07.841 --> 00:27:10.270
these images for learning and we want

