00:55:26.336 --> 00:55:29.230
Okay. So, people have

00:55:29.231 --> 00:55:30.430
thought about
reinforcement learning in

00:55:30.431 --> 00:55:31.690
healthcare and in fact the rise

00:55:31.691 --> 00:55:34.600
freedom papers that
apply off policy RL to

00:55:34.601 --> 00:55:38.260
exactly this problem where
your goal is essentially to

00:55:38.261 --> 00:55:42.040
manage a few different things.

00:55:42.041 --> 00:55:44.380
The most, the simplest version

00:55:44.381 --> 00:55:49.375
of the problem that people have
considered is dosing vasopressors,

00:55:49.376 --> 00:55:50.890
which is a drug that artificially

00:55:50.891 --> 00:55:52.840
increases the blood pressure
of the patient.

00:55:52.841 --> 00:55:55.150
So, what happens often
is that a patient

00:55:55.151 --> 00:55:58.270
that has sepsis or a lot
of people in the ICU in

00:55:58.271 --> 00:56:02.290
general have breathing failure

00:56:02.291 --> 00:56:05.095
and are put on
mechanical ventilation,

00:56:05.096 --> 00:56:07.060
which means that they
might have to be sedated

00:56:07.061 --> 00:56:09.340
to cope with the pain of intubation,

00:56:09.341 --> 00:56:11.800
which means that their blood
pressure might go down and then

00:56:11.801 --> 00:56:14.260
you have to artificially
sort of tweak that.

00:56:14.261 --> 00:56:17.620
So, what is the right dosage

00:56:17.621 --> 00:56:19.390
of vasopressors and
when should you take

00:56:19.391 --> 00:56:20.560
them off because it can't have

00:56:20.561 --> 00:56:24.760
long-lasting or long-term
adverse effects.

00:56:24.761 --> 00:56:28.690
It's an interesting problem. Now, if

00:56:28.691 --> 00:56:31.300
you do this or if you
solve this problem,

00:56:31.301 --> 00:56:34.090
how should we evaluate the
policies that we learned.

00:56:34.091 --> 00:56:37.855
Essentially, if we come up with
something using a false URL,

00:56:37.856 --> 00:56:41.050
we will get some policy out and
we want to have to figure out

00:56:41.051 --> 00:56:42.760
should we apply it in practice and

00:56:42.761 --> 00:56:44.500
we have all the same problems
that we had before.

00:56:44.501 --> 00:56:45.640
We have the overlap problem, we have

00:56:45.641 --> 00:56:48.655
the ignorability problem,
but it's just much,

00:56:48.656 --> 00:56:51.420
much worse now because
essentially where you could

00:56:51.421 --> 00:56:54.570
consider this the overlap between
all of these different choices,

00:56:54.571 --> 00:56:58.800
or if this is the policy that
you might expect in practice,

00:56:58.801 --> 00:57:02.230
at least we want to know what
happens close to this policy.

00:57:04.800 --> 00:57:12.805
But I know there's a lot of research
on specifically this question,

00:57:12.806 --> 00:57:15.475
that the goal that you're
typically interested in is

00:57:15.476 --> 00:57:17.440
this value of the policy which is

00:57:17.441 --> 00:57:20.780
just the expected long-term reward.

00:57:21.330 --> 00:57:23.740
I think I'll skip ahead a little bit.

00:57:23.741 --> 00:57:25.660
But you know essentially that

00:57:25.661 --> 00:57:30.400
this overlap problem becomes
worse the longer my horizon is,

00:57:30.401 --> 00:57:32.890
and I've illustrated
that here just by

00:57:32.891 --> 00:57:35.260
showing that if you were to do

00:57:35.261 --> 00:57:37.240
important sampling in
a setting like this,

00:57:37.241 --> 00:57:38.440
essentially what you're looking for

00:57:38.441 --> 00:57:41.920
is patients in the data
that are treated exactly in

00:57:41.921 --> 00:57:43.825
the way that your proposed policy

00:57:43.826 --> 00:57:46.930
prescribes and the further
your horizon goes,

00:57:46.931 --> 00:57:50.095
this group just get
smaller and smaller.

00:57:50.096 --> 00:57:54.025
So, something called the effective
sample size is exactly this.

00:57:54.026 --> 00:57:57.190
The number of people that you
were left with at the end,

00:57:57.191 --> 00:57:59.960
even if this was
your original sample.

00:58:01.740 --> 00:58:06.715
Recently, I was involved in
writing an opinion paper on this,

00:58:06.716 --> 00:58:10.540
and there are a few problems
of course that you run

00:58:10.541 --> 00:58:12.400
into that seemed to have been glossed

00:58:12.401 --> 00:58:14.815
over in the literature
in a lot of cases.

00:58:14.816 --> 00:58:16.450
So, this paper was mostly

00:58:16.451 --> 00:58:24.445
a warning sign or a warning rather
for these kinds of analyses.

00:58:24.446 --> 00:58:26.320
We know that important sampling has

00:58:26.321 --> 00:58:28.890
very high variance because of
the important sampling problem.

00:58:28.891 --> 00:58:31.410
It's also the case that some

00:58:31.411 --> 00:58:36.050
treatment regimes or some policies
we cannot evaluate at all.

00:58:36.051 --> 00:58:38.110
We also have the problem
that regression is

00:58:38.111 --> 00:58:39.700
often biased and there's a lot of

00:58:39.701 --> 00:58:41.500
good work on trying to
figure out how should we

00:58:41.501 --> 00:58:44.365
interpolate between
these two different methods.

00:58:44.366 --> 00:58:48.730
Confounding problem is
made worse in the sense

00:58:48.731 --> 00:58:52.900
that we have now confounding
variables that are time-dependent,

00:58:52.901 --> 00:58:58.030
and so, the choice of a medication

00:58:58.031 --> 00:59:00.610
later might depend on the choice
that we made earlier.

00:59:00.611 --> 00:59:01.990
A very common thing
is that you don't try

00:59:01.991 --> 00:59:04.540
the same thing twice if it
didn't work the first time,

00:59:04.541 --> 00:59:07.060
and we have to deal
with these things.

00:59:07.061 --> 00:59:10.720
So, this is something that
has happened recently.

00:59:10.721 --> 00:59:15.025
People have looked or researchers
that have published in

00:59:15.026 --> 00:59:16.900
prestigious places have considered

00:59:16.901 --> 00:59:20.664
various heuristic methods
of evaluating policies,

00:59:20.665 --> 00:59:26.710
and I hope at this point in
the talk you will follow in

00:59:26.711 --> 00:59:30.070
my explanation of why this
is not the right thing to

00:59:30.071 --> 00:59:34.825
do, and this is an example.

00:59:34.826 --> 00:59:38.125
Essentially, what you have
here on the y axis is

00:59:38.126 --> 00:59:41.530
the mortality of a patient
in your dataset and on

00:59:41.531 --> 00:59:43.600
the x-axis is how different was

00:59:43.601 --> 00:59:46.210
the action that we

00:59:46.211 --> 00:59:50.050
prescribed to that patient
from what was in the data.

00:59:50.051 --> 00:59:53.530
What they see is that well,
we have this U-shape here

00:59:53.531 --> 00:59:56.950
that says that if you're
far away from our policy,

00:59:56.951 --> 00:59:59.630
then your mortality is higher,

00:59:59.880 --> 01:00:04.315
and you can see why this
goes wrong hopefully.

01:00:04.316 --> 01:00:10.780
But this is by no means
representative of any causal effect.

01:00:10.781 --> 01:00:12.850
This difference between
these averages here.

01:00:12.851 --> 01:00:14.530
So, I'll just say,
these have been bent in

01:00:14.531 --> 01:00:16.075
some ways so you can take
averages of patients,

01:00:16.076 --> 01:00:18.770
but it doesn't really
solve the problem.

01:00:18.900 --> 01:00:22.300
This is no causal effect
because what are

01:00:22.301 --> 01:00:23.725
the two interventions that I'm

01:00:23.726 --> 01:00:26.635
comparing here between
these two groups?

01:00:26.636 --> 01:00:28.720
Is there anything
that makes me believe

01:00:28.721 --> 01:00:30.130
that the patient groups that are

01:00:30.131 --> 01:00:33.010
up here in this dot is
comparable to this group here?

01:00:33.011 --> 01:00:35.620
No. These are completely
different patients.

01:00:35.621 --> 01:00:38.020
Anyone who's treated like this is

01:00:38.021 --> 01:00:40.390
probably somewhat of an easy case.

01:00:40.391 --> 01:00:44.800
Whereas, here you could have patients
that are very extreme cases.

01:00:44.801 --> 01:00:47.725
For example, one reason
that you might have

01:00:47.726 --> 01:00:52.660
a lower than expected treatment

01:00:52.661 --> 01:00:55.120
in your data or
lower than expected dosage,

01:00:55.121 --> 01:00:57.670
might be that the patient
is already too late.

01:00:57.671 --> 01:00:58.945
There's nothing we can do,

01:00:58.946 --> 01:01:01.615
and we can't tell that
from this plot at all.

01:01:01.616 --> 01:01:05.410
Or it might be that if

01:01:05.411 --> 01:01:09.235
you prescribe a- I'm not going
to go on, you get the picture.

01:01:09.236 --> 01:01:12.400
So, you can get the same figure.

01:01:12.401 --> 01:01:14.995
I didn't put the actual plot here,

01:01:14.996 --> 01:01:17.035
but you can get the same figure
for a random policy.

01:01:17.036 --> 01:01:20.335
You can get the same figure for
a constant policy in some cases.

01:01:20.336 --> 01:01:22.030
So, this is purely an effect of

01:01:22.031 --> 01:01:23.350
the the bias in your data and

01:01:23.351 --> 01:01:25.795
not at all about
the quality of the policy.

01:01:25.796 --> 01:01:27.880
So, this was mostly to illustrate

01:01:27.881 --> 01:01:29.485
that this is something we
had to think a lot about.

01:01:29.486 --> 01:01:31.630
I don't have a perfect solution
for you by any means,

01:01:31.631 --> 01:01:32.770
but I thought that
that could be useful

01:01:32.771 --> 01:01:34.880
to see if you haven't already.

01:01:35.460 --> 01:01:39.130
The takeaway here is essentially
that off-policy RL is only

01:01:39.131 --> 01:01:40.640
harder than the causal effect of

01:01:40.641 --> 01:01:42.445
the problem that I
talked about before.

01:01:42.446 --> 01:01:46.420
Overlap is aggravated and ignored
ability is harder to verify.

